<code>assert c != null => this.size() == c.size() && \forall int i; 0 <= i &amp;&amp; i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]);</code>
--------------------
To generate the assert statement for the given comment, we need to ensure that if the collection `c` is not null, then the constructed `ArrayList` is equal to the collection `c`. Since we don't have a direct method to compare two collections for equality in terms of their elements and order, we can infer that the `ArrayList` should have the same size as the collection and that each element at a specific index in the `ArrayList` should be equal to the element at the same index in the collection.

Here's the assert statement that captures this specification:

<code>assert c != null => (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]));</code>

This assert statement checks that for every index `i` from `0` to `this.size() - 1`, the element at index `i` in the `ArrayList` is equal to the element at index `i` in the array representation of the collection `c`.
